---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import SectionNav from "../components/SectionNav.astro";
import LevelSection from "../components/LevelSection.astro";
import { levels, type NodeType } from "../data/levels";

// Each device type is introduced once, above the first level that uses it —
// which is what the pop-up hints used to do, except now it stays on the page.
const seen = new Set<NodeType>();
const sections = levels.map((level, index) => {
  const newDevices = [...new Set(level.nodes.map((node) => node.type))].filter(
    (type) => !seen.has(type),
  );
  for (const type of newDevices) {
    seen.add(type);
  }
  const nextLevel = levels[index + 1];
  return {
    level,
    newDevices,
    next: nextLevel ? { id: nextLevel.id, title: nextLevel.title } : null,
  };
});
---

<Layout title="Can You Break the Internet?">
  <Header />
  <SectionNav />

  <main>
    <section id="intro" class="intro">
      <p class="intro-lead">
        The internet doesn't survive because nothing breaks. It survives because things are allowed
        to break: a link fails, and traffic takes another way round.
      </p>

      <div class="diagram-panel intro-diagram">
        <svg
          class="network"
          data-intro-network
          viewBox="0 0 100 66"
          preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Sample network diagram: a client reaches a server through two possible paths. When the faster path breaks, traffic automatically reroutes through the other, then the path repairs and the cycle repeats."
        ></svg>
      </div>

      <h2 class="intro-heading">How to play</h2>
      <ul class="intro-list">
        <li>
          Every level below is a network you can attack.
          <strong>Click any link to cut it.</strong> The diagram reroutes live and the status line
          shows what's still standing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keyboard:</strong> focus a diagram, use the arrow keys to move between devices and
          links, then press Enter or Space to cut.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Touchscreen:</strong> pinch to zoom, and drag with a thumb to move around.</li>
        <li>
          A level is complete once every route to every server is gone. Levels get harder as the
          networks get closer to the real thing.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="intro-heading">Reading the diagram</h2>
      <ul class="intro-list">
        <li>
          The dark dot is your request, travelling out. Only once it lands does the hollow dot set
          off back: the answer, worked out as its own independent trip rather than the outbound path
          played backwards. (In these diagrams the cheapest way back happens to be the cheapest way
          out, reversed. Real IP traffic isn't guaranteed that.)
        </li>
        <li>The ms next to each link is a simulated one-way delay, not a measurement of any real connection.</li>
        <li>
          A real page load is rarely just one round trip. A browser fires off many requests in
          parallel, and some are answered straight from a cache.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="intro-heading">What this is, and isn't</h2>
      <p>
        These are simplified teaching models, not a literal map of how the internet works. Real
        networks carry far more redundancy and nuance than five diagrams can show. Even a
        well-built one isn't immune to outages: redundancy lowers the odds of a single point of
        failure, but it doesn't guarantee uptime.
      </p>
    </section>

    {sections.map((props) => <LevelSection {...props} />)}
  </main>

  <script src="../scripts/introDiagram.ts"></script>
  <script src="../scripts/main.ts"></script>
</Layout>
